  {"flag": "Large related-party financing recorded as debt through failed sale-leasebacks", "evidence": "AI infrastructure hardware sale-leasebacks with Valor (whose CEO, Antonio Gracias, is a company director) failed sale-leaseback accounting and are carried as debt: $13,329M at 2026-06-30 vs $4,507M at 2025-12-31. Interest expense on these was $327M in Q2 and $513M in H1. The related assets sit in PP&E, and $3,921M of PP&E purchases were financed by other financings (non-cash). Reported cash capex therefore understates total asset additions.", "source": "SEC 10-Q (2026-08-04), Notes 9, 17 and cash flow supplemental disclosures"},
  {"flag": "Internal contradiction on debt maturities", "evidence": "The MD&A says 'no material debt principal payments are due until July 15, 2031', but the Note 9 maturity table shows $944M for the remainder of 2026, $2,402M in 2027, $2,867M in 2028, $3,422M in 2029 and $3,597M in 2030. Current debt is $2,525M.", "source": "SEC 10-Q (2026-08-04), MD&A Debt Agreements vs Note 9"},
  {"flag": "Inconsistent disclosure of SpaceX Notes redemption spread", "evidence": "Note 9 states a spread of 20 to 30 basis points; MD&A states 'between 0.002 and 0.003 basis points'. This points to a drafting or control error in a newly public filer.", "source": "SEC 10-Q (2026-08-04), Note 9 vs MD&A Debt Agreements"},
  {"flag": "Covenant technical default and waiver", "evidence": "The company had a technical default under the SpaceX Credit Facility when it acquired xAI on 2026-02-02 because of subsidiary-level debt assumed. A waiver was obtained on 2026-03-02, and the debt was then refinanced via the bridge loan.", "source": "SEC 10-Q (2026-08-04), Note 9 Covenants"},
