 "bear_case": [
  "Valuation needs several heroic steps to work. At $2.2T the stock trades at about 96x TTM sales ($23.0B) and about 19x even the FY2027 consensus of $113.5B. Management's $1T-by-2030 target would still be priced at about 2.2x sales, and that is the implied bull case. Reaching a normal multiple means roughly 40x revenue growth AND a swing from a negative operating margin to a high-teens or better net margin. FCF is negative, so a DCF cannot be run. The price rests entirely on narrative and scarce float.",
  "Cash burn is accelerating, not stabilizing. FCF was +$0.1B in 2023, -$5.4B in 2024, -$14.1B in 2025 and about -$25.0B in H1 2026 alone, which annualizes to about -$50B. The CFO guides Q2-level capex of about $18.4B per quarter for two more quarters, so FY2026 capex is about $65B, roughly 3x FY2025. H1 capex ($28.5B) was about 6x H1 Adjusted EBITDA ($4.7B). Operating cash flow of $3.5B included +$2.2B of deferred revenue, so ex-prepayments it was only about $1.3B.",
  "Net cash of about $60B ($100.0B cash vs about $39.4B debt) is not the cushion it looks like. H2 capex is about $37B. Spectrum cash payments to EchoStar are still to come. Non-cancelable obligations are $28.0B, of which $22.2B falls in 2027. Debt amortization is $0.9B in the rest of 2026, $2.4B in 2027 and $2.9B in 2028. The 10GW-by-end-2027 target (from about 1.4GW now and 2GW by end-2026) implies far more capex than the cash on hand. That points to more debt, more related-party sale-leasebacks or more equity dilution. Even at an optimistic $20-25B per GW (my estimate), 8 more GW costs $160-200B.",
  "The headline growth is flattered by one new, concentrated, cancellable contract stream. About $1.6B of the Q2 step-up came from AI cloud contracts that started in Q2. They are terminable on 90 days' notice after the ramp and recognized on a 'ramp' pattern. Ex-this stream, Q2 revenue was about $6.2B, so growth was about 52% YoY rather than 92%. Customers A and B are about 18-20% of revenue each (about $2.9B combined if both are consolidated shares) and are unnamed. A concurrent Cursor compute agreement and acquisition option, valued at zero and payable in SPCX stock, creates potential circularity.",
  "AI economics do not support the 'sub-one-year payback, like COGS' framing. The AI segment lost $3.7B at the operating line in H1 on $23.6B of AI capex. Q2 revenue grew by about $3.1B QoQ while the consolidated operating loss narrowed by about $1.8B, from $(1.94)B in Q1 to $(0.14)B in Q2. That is an implausibly high incremental margin for GPU rental. It suggests compute cost is being booked in R&D (AI R&D is $4.6B vs AI cost of revenue of $1.6B), or that capacity is being shifted from internal Grok training. The allocation method is undisclosed. Hardware is Nvidia-only, so SpaceX is a price-taker on its largest input.",
  "Core 'moat' segments are weaker than the narrative. Starlink ARPU fell 22% to $66 as subscribers doubled. At 12M subscribers x $66 the consumer base yields only about $2.4B per quarter of the $4.3B Connectivity revenue, so growth leans on enterprise/government. Space revenue fell 1.9% in H1. Falcon launches fell from 45 to 37 and mass to orbit fell from 652 to 485 tons. H1's 78 launches and 1,041 tons imply Q1 was about 41 launches and 556 tons, so cadence is falling sequentially. The Space segment lost $1.2B in H1 while Connectivity earned $2.8B. Segment profits depend on undisclosed intersegment launch pricing, so the 'vertical integration' benefit cannot be verified. Starship V3 and Starlink V3 are still unproven at scale.",
  "Governance is about as shareholder-hostile as it gets. Class B carries 10 votes per share (about 85-88% of votes, with no sunset). Musk is removable only by Class B holders. The company is Texas-incorporated with a 3% ownership threshold for derivative suits, mandatory arbitration, a jury waiver and a class-action ban. Related-party exposure includes Valor ($13.3B of debt, with its CEO on the board), Tesla Megapack purchases and the common-control xAI/X merger, which had no independent price discovery. Musk's package grants up to 1B more shares. CalPERS, the NY Comptroller and CII objected publicly.",
  "Heavy dilution is queued. 1,331M performance awards are unvested (about 10% of shares). Another 564M securities were excluded as anti-dilutive. Spectrum adds about 261.8M shares and the Cursor merger is $60B in stock (about 358M shares at $167). Musk's package adds up to 1B. If all are issued, share count rises roughly 19-27% (some are conditional). The Spectrum shares are issued at a fixed $42.40 vs a $167.38 market, so the true cost is about $43.8B in equity, not $11.1B. Total consideration is therefore about $52B, not the $19.6B headline.",
  "Technical overhang from the tiny float and unlock schedule. The IPO sold only 638.9M shares (about 4.9% of 13.18B total, about $107B at market). The stock is up 24% from the $135 IPO price on index-inclusion anticipation. Lock-ups expire, Valor has 10b5-1 plans for up to 225.9M shares (about $37.8B) and employee SBC is about $1.5B per half-year. Supply of stock could swamp demand.",
  "Balance sheet quality is questionable. Failed sale-leasebacks with a related party are carried as debt of $13.3B, up 3x in six months. They carry about $513M of H1 interest, a double-digit annualized cost. The credit facility had a technical default in February 2026 that needed a waiver. H1 debt-extinguishment losses were about $1.5B on 9.5%-12.5% legacy debt. Another $3.9B of PP&E was financed outside cash capex and $5.5B sits in payables, so true H1 investment is above $28.5B. Goodwill of $11.1B sits in the loss-making AI segment with no impairment. Only $354M is accrued against Grok class actions, EC DSA fines, Vidstream ($172M) and Clean Air Act claims, with other losses 'not estimable'.",
  "Depreciation is about to swamp any margin gains. D&A already runs about $5.3B per half-year ($2.8B in Q2) before the H2 capex wave. Adding about $37B of H2 capex and a path to 10GW means D&A rises by tens of billions, with GPU obsolescence as the main risk. The consensus FY2027 EPS of $1.94 (about $25B net income, a 22% net margin on $113.5B) looks implausible against a current -7% net margin and rising D&A."
 ],
