e with capital discipline overstate quality. Control by one person with no outside check argues for below average."},{"assumption":"AI compute capital has a sub-one-year payback (CFO).","why_questionable":"Reported numbers do not support it. Q2 AI infrastructure revenue annualizes to about $8.8B on 1.4GW, and the segment lost $3.7B in H1. Even the newly contracted $6.7B ramping in October is small against $18.4B of capex per quarter. The claim is management's own framing and the analysis repeats it without testing it."},{"assumption":"The $100B cash balance provides ample liquidity.","why_questionable":"Most of it is IPO and bond proceeds that are already being spent. Burn of about $18B per quarter of capex, $22.2B of 2027 commitments, debt amortization, spectrum cash and a possible Cursor cash component point to a funding need within about 6 to 8 quarters, before the 10GW target. Management says data-center capex can be cut, but that would undermine the revenue targets that the valuation depends on."},{"assumption":"Backlog of $47.5B and $6.7B of contracted AI cloud revenue provide visibility.","why_questionable":"AI cloud contracts can be terminated on 90 days' notice after the ramp, and revenue is concentrated in a few customers. Backlog of this kind is closer to an option than a commitment. About 44% of backlog is recognized after one year, and the near-term portion covers only a fraction of consensus 2027 revenue."},{"assumption":"Sell-side FY2027 consensus (revenue $113.5B, EPS $1.94) is a usable anchor.","why_questionable":"It implies H2 2026 revenue of about $32B against H1 of $12.5B, which is a doubling per quarter. It has a wide range ($59B to $189B), and it is a revenue and EPS view with no FCF or capex input. A $2.2T cap capitalizes 2027 EPS at 86x, even assuming the targets are met. Analyst estimates after a hot IPO tend to be biased upward."},{"assumption":"Base FCF of -$6.5B (three-year average) used by the valuation code.","why_questionable":"It averages in the 2023 positive FCF and understates the current run-rate by an order of magnitude. H1 2026 FCF was about -$25B and the 2026 run-rate is about -$50B or worse. Using a stale average makes the burn look smaller than it is. The MARKET DATA history may also not be recast for the xAI/X common-control merger, so the 2023-25 series may not be comparable with 2026."},{"assumption":"Operating leverage will appear as revenue scales (Q2 operating loss narrowed to $(143)M).","why_questionable":"The Q2 improvement coincides with new AI revenue that still has ramp-based recognition and uncertain cost allocation. Compute costs are allocated between cost of revenue and R&D at management's discretion. D&A is already $2.8B per quarter and will grow rapidly with $18B per quarter of capex, which will push GAAP margins down even if EBITDA grows."}],"inconsistencies_found":["Market cap versus share count: MARKET DATA 'shares' of 7.696B is Class A only, but the $2.206T market cap equals price times about 13.18B total shares. Any per-share metric based on the 'shares' field is overstated by about 71%. Class A alone would imply about $1.29T.","Debt principal is stated two ways: $38,433M in the key-figures table and $39,512M in the red-flag section, against $39,364M carrying value and MARKET DATA's $39,708M. The totals do not reconcile.","10-Q internal contradiction: MD&A says no material debt principal is due until July 2031, but the Note 9 maturity table shows $944M in the remainder of 2026, $2.4B in 2027, $2.9B in 2028, $3.4B in 2029 and $3.6B in 2030.","SpaceX Notes redemption spread is 20 to 30 bps in Note 9 and '0.002 to 0.003 basis points' in MD&A, which is a drafting or control error.","Customer B is described as 19.5% of Q2 revenue (about $1.5B) in the red flags, but as 19.5% of the AI segment in the risk list. Those are very different: about $1.5B versus about $0.5B. Customer B is possibly the same counterparty that drives the roughly $1.6B AI infrastructure step-up.","Vot